# Chapter 6 — The Buttons

Last chapter the world learned to move on its own — the tick carried a ball,
gravity pulled a drop, a job fired on schedule. But every one of those motions
was decided in advance. You set a velocity and walked away. The world ran, and
you *watched*. What you could not do was reach in and change it while it ran.

This chapter hands you the controller. A button is the console asking you a
question sixty times a second — *are you holding this right now?* — and letting
the answer change what happens on the next tick. The moment your program reads a
button and acts on it, the picture stops being a movie and becomes a game.

## Six buttons, and only six

A Motoi console has a small, fixed gamepad: a four-way pad and two face
buttons. `input/buttons` hands you their names, and it is always the same six:

```motoi
(display (input/buttons))
(newline)
```

```
(up down left right a b)
```

Those symbols — `up`, `down`, `left`, `right`, `a`, `b` — are the *entire*
vocabulary of input. There is no `start`, no `select`, no mouse. That is on
purpose: six buttons is a whole language of play, and a small fixed set means a
game written on one Motoi runs on every Motoi. Any name that is not one of the
six is simply not a button, and the console will say so plainly in a moment.

## Two questions you can ask a button

Here is the idea the whole chapter turns on. There are **two** different things
you might want to know about a button, and they are not the same question:

- **`input/down?`** — *is it held right now?* True for as long as the button
  stays pressed, frame after frame.
- **`input/pressed?`** — *did it just go down on this exact frame?* True for one
  single frame — the instant of the press — and false ever after, even if you
  keep holding.

`down?` is for things that should keep happening while you hold — walking,
thrusting, charging. `pressed?` is for things that should happen **once** per
push — jumping, firing, confirming a menu. Ask the wrong one and a single jump
button held for half a second launches your hero into orbit.

At the prompt, no hands are on the pad, so both answers are `#f`:

```motoi
(display (list (input/down? 'a) (input/pressed? 'a)))
(newline)
```

```
(#f #f)
```

That `(#f #f)` is the console being honest: nothing is holding `a`, so it is
neither *down* nor *just-pressed*. In a running game the console fills the
button state fresh every frame from the real gamepad, and these same two verbs
start answering `#t` at exactly the right moments. You are reading the pad; at
the prompt the pad is simply at rest.

## Poll the whole pad at once

Because the six names come from `input/buttons`, you can ask about all of them
in one sweep with `map` — no need to name each button by hand:

```motoi
(display (map (lambda (b) (input/down? b)) (input/buttons)))
(newline)
```

```
(#f #f #f #f #f #f)
```

Six buttons, six answers, in button order: `up down left right a b`. Right now
they are all `#f` because the pad is idle, but this one line is the shape of
every input-reading loop you will ever write — walk the buttons, ask each one
its state, and decide what the next tick should do. When a real hand presses
`right`, the fourth slot flips to `#t`, and your loop sees it.

## An unknown button is just false

You will fat-finger a name eventually — type `'start` or `'x` out of habit. The
console does not throw an error for that. A name that is not one of the six is
not a button, so asking about it is always simply `#f`:

```motoi
(display (input/down? 'start))
(newline)
```

```
#f
```

This is the same honesty you met with the framebuffer clipping in Chapter 3 and
the sprite stamp-count in Chapter 4: the console tells you the plain truth
rather than blowing up. `start` is not held — because `start` cannot be held,
because there is no such button. If a control seems dead, check the name against
the six first.

## May I read the pad at all?

One last verb rounds out the picture. Sometimes the console is not yours to read
— a menu owns the input, or the game is paused, or a host has taken the pad.
`input/may-i?` answers whether input is yours right now:

```motoi
(display (input/may-i?))
(newline)
```

```
#t
```

At the bare prompt the answer is `#t` — nothing is standing between you and the
pad. In a full console a pause screen or a modal card can flip this to `#f`, and
a well-behaved game checks `input/may-i?` before it acts on a button, so it does
not steer the hero while the player is busy in a menu. It is a small courtesy
that keeps control where the player expects it.

## What you just proved

You met the whole gamepad — six buttons, no more — and learned the one
distinction that separates a jump that fires once from a jump that never stops:
`input/down?` asks *held now*, `input/pressed?` asks *just went down*. You
polled the whole pad in a single line, saw that an unknown name is calmly `#f`,
and learned to ask the console's permission before you read. Every program ran
and answered truthfully; at rest, the truth is a row of `#f`.

You can now *read* the player. What is left is to wire that reading to motion —
to say *when `right` is down, push the hero right* — and let the tick from
Chapter 5 carry the push. That wiring is the next chapter, where buttons,
velocity, and the heartbeat finally meet.

> **Tip**
>
> When a control feels wrong, ask yourself *held or edge?* first. A character
> that slides forever after one tap is almost always a `down?` where you wanted
> `pressed?`; a menu that skips three items on one press is a `pressed?` where
> you wanted a one-frame edge you accidentally read as `down?`. Naming the
> question you meant fixes most input bugs before you touch anything else.

> **Warning**
>
> At the prompt, every button reads `#f` — there is no hand on the pad and
> nothing pumps the frame, so `input/down?` and `input/pressed?` cannot show you
> a `#t` here. This is not a broken button; it is an idle one. To *feel* a
> button respond you run inside a live console, where the frame loop fills the
> pad state each tick. Appendix G lays out the keyboard-to-button mapping and
> the full input model.

